<p>
An old joke, known and beloved by every developer, is &ldquo;A programmer is a machine for turning coffee into code.&rdquo; It&rsquo;s certainly true that it&rsquo;s an important component of my productivity, coding and otherwise. An overwhelming proportion of Irreal readers, I&rsquo;m sure, have a similar story.
</p>
<p>
It turns out that we&rsquo;re on to something. <a href="https://sciencex.com/news/2026-04-coffee-doesnt-key-biological-pathway.html">It&rsquo;s not just that coffee gives us a boost—everyone knows that—but that coffee is actually good for you</a>. That&rsquo;s also been known for some time but less widely. For some time, coffee consumption has been correlated with longer life, and less risk of chronic diseases.
</p>
<p>
Now scientists think they understand why. They believe that the compounds in coffee activate the NR4A1 receptor a protein associated with aging, stress, and disease. It&rsquo;s not just the caffeine that does the trick. Several coffee compounds are involved, which explains why decaffeinated coffee also offers the health benefits if not the boost of regular coffee.
</p>
<p>
In the mean time, the researchers don&rsquo;t recommend any changes to your habits concerning coffee consumption. Take a look at the linked <a href="https://sciencex.com">Science X</a> report for more details on how the benefits work and how the researchers believe that there are almost certainly other receptors involved.
</p>
<p>
At the end of the day, I suppose this doesn&rsquo;t make a lot of difference. Programmers will continue slurping coffee but it&rsquo;s nice to know that you can be healthy without <a href="https://condenaststore.com/featured/its-broccoli-carl-rose.html">eating spinach</a>.</p>
